This file is a placeholder. The real one is a Google service account key and is
untracked (see .gitignore) — it can publish releases in the developer's name, so
it belongs on the release machine and nowhere else.

To create it, once:

  1. Google Cloud Console → create (or pick) a project → APIs & Services →
     enable "Google Play Android Developer API".
  2. IAM & Admin → Service Accounts → create one. No project roles are needed;
     the permissions that matter are granted in the Play Console, not here.
  3. On the service account → Keys → Add key → Create new key → JSON.
     Download it.
  4. Play Console → Users and permissions → Invite new user → paste the service
     account's e-mail address. Grant it, for this app only:
       • Release to testing tracks
       • Release to production  (only if you want automated production pushes)
       • View app information
  5. Save the downloaded JSON next to this file as:
       android-app/play-service-account.json

Then:

  ./gradlew publishBundle                    # internal track
  ./gradlew publishBundle --track production # explicit, never the default

Note that the Play Developer API cannot create the FIRST release of an app.
Google requires one bundle to be uploaded through the Play Console by hand
before the API will accept anything for that package name.

Rotating the key: delete it under the service account's Keys tab and create a
new one. The Play Console invitation stays valid — it is bound to the service
account, not to the key.
